 \begin{abstract}
-Editing the action context of an autoregressive vision--language--action model (VLA) can change its later decoded actions, its executed motion, and its task success by very different amounts. We measure these levels separately, using context interventions that leave already emitted actions unchanged, with model-specific no-op and replay checks. In AutoVLA driving plans, replacing a ground-truth latest motion token with a frozen model token increases severe open-loop trajectory events by 25.5 percentage points (pp) in 100 failure-selected scenes, also behind a natural older token; repeated-correction effects recur on new drives, and design-weighted eligible-frame effects are about 4\,pp. In VLA-0, which emits actions as integer text, editing the action vector immediately before the continuation changes the continuation more than editing the vector before it (normalized difference 0.0137, 95\% task-cluster interval $[0.0115,0.0157]$; 149 observations, 30 LIBERO tasks). In paired rollouts through VLA-0's official ensemble loop, such context-only edits first change the executed command three steps after the first edited call and change end-effector paths by 0.8--9.2\,mm after 48 steps in four task--state units. SpatialVLA rollouts show physical deviations while task-equal success effects are bounded only within $\pm10$\,pp. AutoVLA and VLA-0 share a Qwen2.5-VL-3B backbone. In SpatialVLA (SigLIP/Gemma~2), a verified within-call path with pre-specified distance-matched donors again yields a larger response to the latest than to the older preceding action (normalized difference 0.048, 95\% layout-bootstrap interval $[0.026,0.072]$; 69 observations, 34 layouts, two SimplerEnv tasks). The pattern is thus observed in a second backbone family, without isolating a backbone effect.
