 \section{Discussion and Limitations}
 \label{sec:limits}
-\textbf{What the levels show.} Controlled context edits produce position-specific decoding responses in three policies with different action representations, domains, and two backbone families; the AutoVLA latest-position contrast and the direct latest-versus-older comparisons in VLA-0 and SpatialVLA are different estimands (Supplementary Sec.~S). In VLA-0 these responses reach executed commands and end-effector motion through the official loop; in SpatialVLA, physical deviation coexists with task-equal success effects bounded within $\pm10$\,pp. Decoding response, motion, and success must therefore be measured separately. The three-step VLA-0 delay verifies the intervention path rather than revealing a learned property.
+\textbf{What the levels show.} Controlled context edits produce position-specific decoding responses in three policies with different action representations, domains, and two backbone families; the AutoVLA latest-position contrast and the direct latest-versus-older comparisons in VLA-0 and SpatialVLA are different estimands (Supplementary Sec.~S). In VLA-0 these responses reach executed commands and end-effector motion through the official loop; in SpatialVLA's separate cross-step study, physical deviation coexists with task-equal success effects bounded within $\pm10$\,pp. Decoding response, motion, and success must therefore be measured separately. The three-step VLA-0 delay verifies the intervention path rather than revealing a learned property.
 
 \textbf{Limitations.} AutoVLA and VLA-0 share Qwen2.5-VL-3B. SpatialVLA extends the within-call comparison to SigLIP/Gemma~2 at the command level on two reused simulator tasks plus one supplementary task. Observing the pattern across backbones does not isolate a backbone effect, because the policies also differ in data, action representation, and domain. Among surveyed alternatives, FAST has temporally nonlocal tokens, original OpenVLA predicts one step per call, OFT decodes in parallel, and WorldVLA masks preceding action blocks (Supplementary Sec.~T). AutoVLA results are five-second open-loop plans with GT-conditioned contrasts; they do not establish closed-loop driving failures or a deployable GT-free recovery method, and correcting toward a self-generated reference can worsen GT agreement (an earlier failure-selected analysis: $+19.7$\,pp severe events). Weighted frame effects are much smaller than failure-selected ones, and shared days and vehicles leave six components without independent day-and-vehicle validation. The VLA-0 rollouts cover four task--state units over 48 steps and do not estimate success; the pilot's observations are initial states. The SpatialVLA cross-step outcome study covers two tasks with reused layouts. Impromptu VLA~\cite{chi2025impromptu} and OpenVLA analyses lack intervention-path parity evidence and remain descriptive (Supplementary Sec.~G). Several interval and visual analyses are post hoc and are labeled as such; retained-window and transient-exposure analyses of GT correction are in Supplementary Sec.~I.
 